## 81. Grid Formula Summary

### Squares

```text
\boxed{
\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}6
}
```

### Rectangles

```text
\boxed{
\frac{m(m+1)n(n+1)}4
}
```

### Rectangles excluding squares

```text
\boxed{
\text{Rectangles}-\text{Squares}
}
```

for square grids when the question excludes squares.

---

## 82. Grid Formula Chart

| Grid | Squares | Rectangles |
|---|---:|---:|
| 1×1 | 1 | 1 |
| 2×2 | 5 | 9 |
| 3×3 | 14 | 36 |
| 4×4 | 30 | 100 |
| 5×5 | 55 | 225 |
| 6×6 | 91 | 441 |

Note:

For a 2×2 grid:

```text
{3\choose2}^2=9
```

rectangles, including squares.

---

## 83. Figure Counting Using a Table

For complex diagrams, make:

| Figure | Small | Medium | Large | Composite | Total |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Triangle | | | | | |
| Square | | | | | |
| Rectangle | | | | | |
| Other | | | | | |

This reduces errors.

---

## 84. Triangle Counting Table

| Type | Up | Down | Total |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Small | | | |
| Medium | | | |
| Large | | | |
| Composite | | | |

Then:

```text
T=U+D
```

---

## 85. Counting by Direction

For complex triangles:

```text
UPWARD
▲

DOWNWARD
▼

LEFT
◀

RIGHT
▶
```

Count each orientation independently.

---

## 86. The Rotation Rule

If a figure can be rotated into another position but its boundary is identical:

> It is still a separate occurrence if it occupies a different location in the diagram.

Do not merge physically different figures just because they have the same shape.

---

## 87. Congruent vs Same Figure

Two triangles can be congruent:

```text
△   △
```

but still count as two separate triangles if they occupy different positions.

Classification of shape and counting of occurrence are different concepts.

---

## 88. Nested Figures

Example:

```text
┌─────────┐
│ ┌─────┐ │
│ │ ┌─┐ │ │
│ │ └─┘ │ │
│ └─────┘ │
└─────────┘
```

There are:

- outer rectangle
- middle rectangle
- inner rectangle

Count each valid boundary separately.

---

## 89. Shared Vertices

Two figures can share a vertex:

```text
  /\  /\
 /  \/  \
```

The shared point does not merge the figures into one automatically.

Count each closed boundary.

---

## 90. Shared Edges

Two figures can share an edge:

```text
┌───┬───┐
│ A │ B │
└───┴───┘
```

Count:

```text
A
B
A+B
```

when the outer boundary forms a valid rectangle.

---

## 91. Missing-Line Trap

```text
┌───┐
│   │
│   └──
```

Do not count a rectangle if the boundary is incomplete.

---

## 92. Crossing-Line Trap

When lines cross, new vertices are created.

Example:

```text
\ /
 X
/ \
```

The intersection can create additional possible figures.

Always include intersection points.

---

## 93. Intersection Creates New Segments

If:

```text
A────X────B
```

then the original segment AB can be viewed as:

```text
AX
XB
AB
```

So one straight line with 3 marked points gives:

```text
3
```

segments.

---

## 94. Intersection Creates New Triangles

A line crossing a triangle can divide it into multiple smaller triangles.

But the original outer triangle may still remain.

Therefore:

```text
small triangles
+
large triangle
```

must both be considered.

---

## 95. Counting Strategy for Crossed Figures

```text
Step 1 → Mark every intersection
Step 2 → Label vertices
Step 3 → Identify elementary segments
Step 4 → Search smallest figures
Step 5 → Search combinations
Step 6 → Search complete outer figures
Step 7 → Remove duplicates
```

---
